======================================================================
TR-4  FORWARD SECRECY EXPERIMENT
======================================================================

[PHASE 1] Running session S1
[alice] SEND  ctr=0 nonce=000000000000000000000000 aad=435332344230303143533234423030328109340a564cd3ffc48b497eaad000d0f74af88d515854c8bb76118066a1a58d0000000000000000 ct=e3db36d904f5daf33918206eaff217fa... (63B)  SUCCESS
[bob] RECV  ctr=0 nonce=000000000000000000000000 aad=435332344230303143533234423030328109340a564cd3ffc48b497eaad000d0f74af88d515854c8bb76118066a1a58d0000000000000000 plaintext=b'S1 confidential payload -- must stay unreadable'  SUCCESS

[ATTACKER] recorded handshake + 63-byte ciphertext

[PHASE 2] Discarding ephemeral and session secrets (FR-7)
[alice] traffic keys discarded (FR-7)
[bob] traffic keys discarded (FR-7)
    ephemeral X25519 private keys, shared secret and traffic keys: gone

[PHASE 3] Attacker compromises Alice's long-term Ed25519 private key
    stolen LT private key = 4c0b487e02776131286eae80f804d2d5... (32 bytes)

    Attempting to reconstruct S1 traffic keys from it...
    - Ed25519 is a SIGNATURE key. It has no Diffie-Hellman operation.
    - The traffic keys came from HKDF(X25519(a_eph, B_eph)).
    - Neither ephemeral private key is derivable from the LT key,
      and both were destroyed in Phase 2.
    - Recovering a_eph from the recorded public key is the discrete
      log problem on Curve25519.

    >>> RECONSTRUCTION FAILED -- S1 remains confidential.  SUCCESS

[PHASE 4] Controlled comparison: attacker instead has the OLD
          ephemeral X25519 private key (i.e. Phase 2 never happened)
    recovered plaintext: b'S1 confidential payload -- must stay unreadable'
    >>> RECOVERY SUCCEEDED -- confirms the ephemeral key, not the
        long-term key, is what protects past traffic.

======================================================================
TR-4 RESULT
======================================================================

Forward secrecy holds because the session keys depend only on ephemeral
X25519 secrets that exist for one session and are then destroyed. The
long-term Ed25519 key authenticates WHO sent the ephemeral public keys;
it never contributes entropy to the keys themselves.

So an attacker who records traffic today and steals the identity key
tomorrow gains the ability to IMPERSONATE Alice in future sessions, but
gains nothing against past ones. Phase 4 isolates the variable: the same
ciphertext falls immediately to the ephemeral private key, proving the
Phase 3 failure is due to the deletion of that specific secret and not to
any accident of the setup.

Contrast with static RSA key transport, where the long-term key decrypts
the premaster secret: there, one key compromise retroactively opens every
recorded session.

